INT. FUNICULAR TICKET HOUSE - NIGHT

Closed for the season. Rain on the glass. A brass ticket machine. A space heater ticking orange.

LOLA ITURBE, thirties, wool coat, soaked hair, sits on the counter with a thermos. Alone. She stares at the dead cable car through the window.

She sips. Checks her watch. Sips.

The door BANGS open. Wind. Wet leaves.

JULIAN ASANTE-WRIGHT, late thirties, rain-slicked trench, a suitcase in one hand. He stops dead.

JULIAN
You're not supposed to be here.

LOLA
Neither are you.

He shuts the door. It takes two tries. She watches him fight it. He doesn't say thanks.

JULIAN
The last train left at nine.

LOLA
Eight forty.

JULIAN
Eight forty.

He sets the suitcase on the floor. Looks at the heater. She slides the thermos along the counter. He doesn't take it.

JULIAN (CONT'D)
Is that coffee?

LOLA
Tea.

JULIAN
Right.

He takes it. Sips. Grimaces.

JULIAN (CONT'D)
That's awful.

LOLA
It's chamomile. It's meant to be.

She smiles. Barely. He almost does.

He sits on the bench by the wall. She stays on the counter. Rain drums.

JULIAN
How long have you been here?

LOLA
Since six.

JULIAN
Why?

LOLA
I had a ticket.

JULIAN
For the nine o'clock?

LOLA
For the one last year.

He looks at her. Slowly she takes a crumpled ticket from her pocket. Holds it up, a stub, brown at the corners.

He recognises it. His jaw tightens.

JULIAN
Lola.

LOLA
You never came.

JULIAN
I wrote.

LOLA
You wrote a postcard. Of a lighthouse.

JULIAN
It was a nice lighthouse.

She laughs, short and sharp. He doesn't.

LOLA
It's a year, Julian. A year since I stood on that platform.

JULIAN
I know.

LOLA
No, you don't. You were in Lisbon.

JULIAN
I was in a hospital.

Silence. The heater ticks.

LOLA
What?

JULIAN
My mother. She had a stroke the night before. I couldn't--

LOLA
You never said.

JULIAN
You never asked.

She slides off the counter. Crosses to him. He stands.

LOLA
I waited here. Alone. I thought you'd--

JULIAN
Stop.

LOLA
I thought you'd chosen not to--

JULIAN
Lola, stop.

She doesn't. He steps closer. Her hands are shaking. So are his.

LOLA
You could have called.

JULIAN
She was dying. I was holding her hand!

LOLA
(louder)
And I was holding a ticket!

The words hang. Rain slams the glass. Both breathe hard.

He reaches out. She slaps his hand away. He catches her wrist anyway. Neither pulls back.

JULIAN
She's alive.

LOLA
(stunned)
What?

JULIAN
She recovered. She asks about you every week.

Lola's face crumples. She sags against him, forehead to his collar. His arms close around her. The suitcase tips over. Neither notices.

LOLA
(muffled)
Idiot.

JULIAN
Yes.

He rests his chin on her hair. She grips his coat in both fists.

The funicular lights flicker. A distant mechanical hum starts up. The cable car, far above, begins to move.

They both lift their heads and stare.

JULIAN (CONT'D)
That's not scheduled.

LOLA
(wiping her face)
Then let's see where it goes.
